-- Castle Nathria (map 2296) -- creature SPAWN rows, transcoded from the Shadowlands-era
-- source dump (SLDB_902_world_2021_03_12.sql) filtered to map=2296, plus a same-guid
-- position correction from 2021_03_16_08_world_altimor.sql (Huntsman Altimor, later-dated,
-- supersedes the main dump's position for that spawn). 2021_03_18_00_world_darkvein.sql,
-- 2021_03_15_01_world_sl_dung.sql (map 2285, not this raid) and the large
-- 2021_03_18_02_world_cre_gob_data.sql supplement were checked and contain no relevant
-- creature SPAWN rows (template/script-level data only) -- not applied here.
--
-- GUID remap: source guids are a scattered/unrelated id space (plain small ints in the
-- main dump, 16-digit ids in the boss-specific patches) and are NOT reused. Fresh guids
-- are assigned sequentially (15000019+) ordered by source guid, so the mapping
-- is deterministic for a given source snapshot.
--
-- spawnMask=1 / phaseMask=1 for all rows (the 4-way SL spawnDifficulties split is
-- intentionally NOT encoded -- single baseline difficulty per prior scope decision).
-- curhealth=1 / curmana=0 for all rows (AC 'not dead on load' sentinel -- the source
-- dump's own curhealth/curmana columns are not reliable snapshot values, see 0 in many
-- source rows). ScriptName='' / VerifiedBuild=NULL for all rows (template-level concern,
-- handled by a companion agent elsewhere). Comment = creature name where resolvable from
-- the source dump's own `creature_template` rows for the known Nathria entry ids, else ''.
--
-- 1515 rows. Apply to acore_world.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- creature
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- DEDUPE (2026-07-19, post first in-game test): the source dump turned out to contain
-- systematically duplicated spawn rows (multiple sniff sessions / difficulty passes merged --
-- up to 18 copies of the same entry at the same coords; 823 of the original 1515 rows were
-- same-entry duplicates within 3 yd). Deduplicated keep-first; guids reassigned sequentially.
-- The DELETE below intentionally still covers the ORIGINAL full guid range 15000019-15001533
-- so re-running this file also purges the duplicate rows from an earlier import.


DELETE FROM `creature` WHERE `guid` BETWEEN 15000019 AND 15001533;


